libkrun: show a screen and map Venus memory with upstream virglrenderer on macOS (Venus only, no vrend).
- 2D resources the renderer refuses stay in guest pages, copied to the display on flush.
- Global-ring fences complete immediately when the renderer has none (2D work is synchronous).
- The cursor queue is serviced (it never was: once full, the guest's display updates blocked
  behind it). Its newest UPDATE_CURSOR goes to the GPU worker, which hands the cursor image to a new
  optional display callback, update_cursor.
- Each input device gets its own id: they all shared one, so only the last (the pointer) reached
  the guest and the keyboard was lost.
- A virtio-snd device (one stereo S16 48 kHz playback stream) whose PCM goes to a host fd as it
  plays, its buffers completed in real time: krun_sound_device_new.
- A freed blob releases its host mapping (destroying a mapped resource leaked its memory for
  the life of the VM).
- Guest RAM on macOS is owned memory in 2 MiB chunks: plain anonymous memory mapped into the
  guest was charged twice once the host wrote into it (every disk read), doubling the VM's
  footprint. The balloon's free-page reporting returns memory to macOS: a reported range leaves
  the guest, gets fresh chunks (freeing the old) and is mapped back (madvise cannot free pages
  mapped into the guest).
- Blob mapping accepts shm exports, which is what upstream Venus produces on macOS.
- SET_SCANOUT_BLOB shows GPU-rendered buffers (copied from the blob's host mapping on flush).
- The VMM worker that maps GPU blobs into the guest is started on macOS (it was never started).
- HVF uses a 4 KiB IPA granule (macOS 26+), so blobs map at the guest's 4 KiB placement on any
  guest kernel.

